  <div class="post-content"><p>Getting a letter from the IRS can be scary, especially if you don't speak English or don't understand what it means. The good news is that most IRS letters don't mean you're in serious trouble — but they do require immediate attention.</p><h2>Don't ignore the letter</h2><p>The most common mistake is not responding. The IRS has strict deadlines. If you ignore a letter, the problem grows and penalties increase. Read the entire letter and look for the response deadline.</p><h2>Identify the type of letter</h2><p>The IRS sends different types of letters. Some are simple notices about changes to your return. Others are audits or debt collections. In the upper right corner of the letter you'll see a number starting with CP or LTR — that number identifies exactly what the IRS wants.</p><h2>Don't respond alone</h2><p>If you have a business, an LLC, or owe money to the IRS, don't try to respond without professional help. An incorrect response can complicate your situation. An accountant experienced with IRS issues can negotiate on your behalf, stop collections, and in many cases reduce the debt.</p><h2>Guzman Tax Accounting is here to help</h2><p>At Guzman Tax Accounting, we've spent years helping Latino families and businesses in Las Vegas resolve IRS issues. We take care of reading the letter, understanding what the IRS is asking for, and responding correctly on your behalf — all in Spanish, with no hidden fees. Call us today at (725) 340-6758 or reach out on WhatsApp. Your first consultation is quick, and we'll give you clear guidance on your situation.</p><a href="/en/consultation" class="btn btn-primary" style="margin-top:8px;">Get a Consultation</a></div>
